  <span class="hero-ornament"></span>
  <h1 class="research-title">History Now</h1>
  <p class="ref-lede">What is being found and written about the past this week, the round anniversaries of the next thirty days, and what is new at the Institute.</p>
</header>

<section class="now-sec now-sec-news" id="news" aria-labelledby="now-news-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-news-t">In the news</h2>
  <p class="now-note">Headlines from other publications, newest first, each with the Institute&rsquo;s page on its subject. The headlines, the articles and the thumbnails their feeds offer belong to their publishers: the Institute has not written or checked them. A headline without a publisher&rsquo;s thumbnail shows the picture of the Institute&rsquo;s page instead. The list is refreshed twice a day.</p>
  <ol class="now-list" data-now="news" data-max="10"><li class="now-wait">The headlines need JavaScript.</li></ol>
  <p class="now-note now-src">From the news pages of <a href="https://archaeology.org/news/" rel="noopener">Archaeology Magazine</a>, <a href="https://www.smithsonianmag.com/history/" rel="noopener">Smithsonian Magazine</a>, <a href="https://phys.org/science-news/archaeology/" rel="noopener">Phys.org</a>, <a href="https://archaeologymag.com/" rel="noopener">Archaeology News</a>, <a href="https://www.medievalists.net/" rel="noopener">Medievalists.net</a>, <a href="https://www.thehistoryblog.com/" rel="noopener">The History Blog</a>. A headline is listed only when it names a page of the Institute.</p>
</section>

<section class="now-sec" id="anniversaries" aria-labelledby="now-ann-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-ann-t">Coming anniversaries</h2>
  <p class="now-note">Round anniversaries in the next thirty days: centuries, half-centuries, quarter-centuries of the last three hundred years and the decades of the last hundred. Every day has its own page in <a href="/today-in-history/">Today in History</a>.</p>
  <ol class="now-list" data-now="ann" data-max="40"><li class="now-wait">The anniversaries need JavaScript.</li></ol>
</section>

<section class="now-sec" id="new" aria-labelledby="now-new-t">
  <h2 class="ref-sec-title" id="now-new-t">New at the Institute</h2>
  <ol class="wn-list">
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-23">23 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Ninety more cities</h3><p class="wn-s">The cities now number 110. New among them: Moscow, Berlin, Vienna, Lisbon, Prague, Kyiv, Florence, Granada, Damascus, Aleppo, Medina, Bukhara, Herat, Persepolis, Nineveh, Lahore, Varanasi, Angkor, Malacca, Nanjing, Hangzhou, Seoul, Lhasa, Karakorum, Djenné, Benin City, Kumasi, Meroë, Mexico City, Potosí, Havana, Boston, Sydney and Honolulu. Each profile tells the city’s origins, its great ages, who ruled it, life in the city, its sieges and disasters, and what it left.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=city">Cities</a> <a href="/city/moscow">Moscow</a> <a href="/city/angkor">Angkor</a> <a href="/city/benin-city">Benin City</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-23">23 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Fifty-one more journeys on the globe</h3><p class="wn-s">Journeys on the Globe now follows 65 journeys stop by stop, 864 stops in all. New among them: Hannibal over the Alps, Faxian, the First Crusade, Rabban Bar Sauma, William of Rubruck, Vasco da Gama, Cortés, Drake, Tasman, Bering, Cook’s second and third voyages, the First Fleet, Humboldt, Mungo Park, Livingstone, the Trail of Tears, the Great Trek, Burke and Wills, the Iwakura Mission, Nansen’s Fram, Amundsen and Scott, Shackleton’s Endurance, the Salt March, Everest in 1953 and Hōkūleʻa. Choose an age first, then a journey.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/journeys">Journeys on the Globe</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-23">23 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Menus by subject</h3><p class="wn-s">The menus are grouped by what you are looking for: People, Places, Events, Ideas, Play and More. No menu is longer than eight items, each ends with a link to its part of Explore Everything, and the footer and Explore Everything follow the same subjects, with cards for the newer sections (great lives, cities, battles, journeys, great works, wars, quotations). On the battle fields, each formation now shows small pictures of what it is: shields, spears, bows, horses, elephants, cannon, muskets, ships.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/research">Explore Everything</a> <a href="/profiles?kind=battle">Battles</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-23">23 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Eighty-five more battles, each with its field in motion</h3><p class="wn-s">The battles now number 103, from Kadesh to Dien Bien Phu: Thermopylae, Salamis, Issus, Zama, Actium, Yarmuk, Manzikert, Crécy, Grunwald, Mohács, Nagashino, Vienna, Poltava, Trafalgar, Austerlitz, Leipzig, Ayacucho, Isandlwana, Tsushima, Verdun, Midway, Kursk, the Normandy landings and many more. Each has its full profile and a plan of the field to play phase by phase. Only battles whose written accounts say how both sides were drawn up are included, so some famous ones are missing.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/profiles?kind=battle">Battles</a> <a href="/battle/battle-of-trafalgar">Battle of Trafalgar</a> <a href="/battle/battle-of-the-yarmuk">Battle of the Yarmuk</a> <a href="/battle/battle-of-nagashino">Battle of Nagashino</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-23">23 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Battles in motion: how each battle was fought</h3><p class="wn-s">Seventeen battle pages have a new section, “How it was fought”: a plan of the field with the ground the accounts name, each formation as a block, and the fight phase by phase. Press Play, or step through with Next and Back. Every plan is drawn from the written accounts and says where a position is a judgement. Two battles whose accounts give no formations, Tours and Red Cliffs, are no longer profiles; their encyclopedia entries stay.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/battle/battle-of-cannae#how-it-was-fought">Battle of Cannae</a> <a href="/battle/battle-of-hastings#how-it-was-fought">Battle of Hastings</a> <a href="/battle/battle-of-ain-jalut#how-it-was-fought">Battle of Ain Jalut</a> <a href="/profiles?kind=battle">All battles</a></p></li>
    <li class="wn-note"><p class="wn-d"><time datetime="2026-11-22">22 November 2026</time></p><h3 class="wn-t">Sources under each section of a profile</h3><p class="wn-s">Under the sections of 620 profiles, “Sources for this section” now lists up to four scholarly works: the books that Wikipedia’s article on the subject cites where it covers the same ground. Each list names the article and the parts of it the works come from.</p><p class="wn-links"><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> <a href="/empire/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> <a href="/method">How the Institute works</a></p></li>
  </ol>
  <p class="now-note"><a href="/new">Everything that is new, and every page added in the last thirty days &rarr;</a></p>
